      "summary": "Check charger type/voltage, charge time, wiring drop, and external loads. After overcharge protection the port reads 0.5-0.7V below full — that's normal; rest 30 min to let the BMS recover.",
      "template": "WHAT'S HAPPENING\nThe battery charges but never reaches full capacity, or the displayed voltage sits below the expected full figure.\n\nHOW TO DIAGNOSE\n1. Charger type — must be LiFePO4 or a device with a lithium mode (MPPT/DCDC).\n2. Charging voltage — within the battery's range (12V: 14.2-14.6V; ×2/×3/×4 for 24/36/48V).\n3. Charge current & time — enough hours; for PV, check light and panel power.\n4. Wiring — long/thin cable causes voltage drop so the charger 'thinks' it's full early.\n5. External load drawing current during charge.\n\nFIX\n- Confirm a correct charger and voltage, shorten/upsize the cable, remove loads, and run a full uninterrupted charge.\n- Charge directly with a LiFePO4 charger to isolate whether it's the battery or the charging device.\n- If the battery triggered overcharge protection, the port voltage reads 0.5-0.7V below the normal full voltage — disconnect all wires/loads for ~30 minutes to let the BMS recover.\n\nWHY IT HAPPENS\nVoltage drop, an underpowered/incorrect charger, or the BMS having ended the charge for protection — not lost capacity.\n\nHOW TO AVOID\nMatch the charger to the pack, keep charging cables short and adequately gauged, and let charges complete without a parallel load.",
