      "template": "WHAT'S HAPPENING\nThe battery runs down much faster than expected, or seems to hold less than its rated capacity.\n\nHOW TO DIAGNOSE\n1. Single battery: fully charge, then record resting voltage at 30min, 1h, 2h, 6h, 12h, 24h. A drop of <=0.05V is normal; more suggests an issue.\n2. Series/parallel system: confirm connections are tight and un-oxidised, then disassemble and repeat the test per battery to find the odd one out.\n3. SoC method: don't judge SoC by voltage. Bluetooth models read SoC in the app; non-Bluetooth need a shunt-based monitor.\n4. Account for inverter conversion losses when estimating runtime.\n5. Check for mixed old/new cells.\n\nFIX\n- Tighten/clean connections, correct the SoC method, recharge fully (parallel-equalise 12-24h), then re-test runtime against the expected figure including inverter efficiency.\n- Only mix batteries of the same brand/model/BMS bought within ~1 month.\n\nWHY IT HAPPENS\nUsually system-level: resistance losses, mis-measured SoC, inverter overhead, or one weaker battery dragging a bank — not necessarily a bad cell.\n\nHOW TO AVOID\nKeep terminals clean and torqued, measure SoC with the right tool, size the inverter sensibly, and don't mix mismatched batteries.",
