      "issue": "Two batteries not balanced / SoC out of sync (parallel)",
      "summary": "Small internal-resistance differences cause uneven current and SoC display gaps. They converge near end of discharge and don't harm the batteries. App parallel systems: upgrade to v2.1.0; or apply a bigger load so total current is high enough.",
      "template": "WHAT'S HAPPENING\nTwo batteries wired together show different SoC (e.g. 80% vs 70%), or one discharges while the other sits at 99-100% 'standby'.\n\nHOW TO DIAGNOSE\n- Confirm they are identical model/BMS and were charged to the same voltage before connecting.\n- Note the load size: at low current the SoC/discharge logic can diverge.\n- Bluetooth systems: check the app version and whether batteries were added as Parallel (P).\n\nFIX\n- SoC gap: it's a display effect from tiny internal-resistance differences. The values converge as discharge nears the end. A full charge/discharge cycle every 3-6 months recalibrates SoC.\n- Non-synced discharge: apply a larger load so total current is high enough for both to engage (e.g. ≥10A, or ≥50A on bigger banks). Or, when battery A reaches ~90% SoC, momentarily turn its discharge off in the app so B starts, then turn A back on.\n- Bluetooth parallel: upgrade the app to v2.1.0 and add the batteries as Parallel (P) — the system then auto-balances discharge (green dot = active, yellow = temporarily protected).\n\nWHY IT HAPPENS\nNo two cells have identical internal resistance, so at low current one battery carries most of the load until voltages equalise. SoC is computed from current, so the readout differs. It is normal parallel behaviour.\n\nHOW TO AVOID\nBefore connecting: same model, charge each to the same voltage, then parallel for 12-24h to equalise. Use adequately-sized, equal-length cabling, and keep the app updated.",
