      "template": "WHAT'S HAPPENING\nVoltage briefly collapses when a load kicks in (AC compressor, inverter inrush, pump), or steps down when charging stops.\n\nHOW TO DIAGNOSE\n1. Does the dip coincide with a high-inrush device starting? Compare its surge current to the battery's continuous/peak rating.\n2. Did a charger just disconnect? Voltage naturally falls from charge voltage back to open-circuit voltage.\n3. Check terminals for looseness, cable oxidation, or high junction resistance.\n\nFIX\n- Reduce the peak load or move to a higher-rated battery/bank for surge headroom.\n- Re-tighten and clean terminals; upsize undersized cable.\n- If it's just the post-charge settle from 14.x to ~13.x V, that's expected, not a fault.\n\nWHY IT HAPPENS\nThe instantaneous load exceeded what the pack/wiring could deliver without sag, or resistance at a joint caused a local drop.\n\nHOW TO AVOID\nSize the battery and cabling for surge loads, not just steady draw, and keep every connection tight and clean.",
