      "summary": "After full charge the BMS opens the charge path; an MPPT/DCDC or charger then shows a 'phantom' voltage (e.g. 14.6V charger reading 19V). Not dangerous — remove the charger or discharge briefly.",
      "template": "WHAT'S HAPPENING\nWhen the battery hits full, the measured voltage jumps unexpectedly high — a 14.6V charger may show ~19V, or an MPPT/DCDC port shows an inflated number.\n\nHOW TO DIAGNOSE\n- Confirm it only happens at/after full charge.\n- Identify what's connected: a charger, MPPT, or DCDC at the moment of the reading.\n- Try discharging slightly and re-measure.\n\nFIX\n- It's a phantom (false) voltage: once full, the BMS closes the internal charge path, and the still-active charging device's open output shows on the terminals. Remove the charger or let the battery discharge a little and the reading normalises.\n- With MPPT/DCDC, the displayed figure is the device's output-port voltage after overcharge protection — discharge to confirm the battery carries a load normally.\n\nWHY IT HAPPENS\nThe BMS has done its job and disconnected charging; the external source's no-load voltage is what the meter sees, not a real cell over-voltage.\n\nHOW TO AVOID\nNothing to fix — it's expected. If it bothers the customer, avoid leaving a charger connected after full, or use a charger that drops to a proper float.",
