      "summary": "SAFETY FIRST: stop, don't keep operating. Disconnect immediately, check for a blown fuse, and inspect for heat/spark damage. Reversed connection can damage the BMS, the charger, and connected devices.",
      "template": "WHAT'S HAPPENING\nThe positive and negative were connected the wrong way round — to a charger, load, or in a series/parallel bank. There may be a spark, a blown fuse, tripped breaker, no output, or a burning smell.\n\nHOW TO DIAGNOSE (safely)\n1. Stop using the system immediately. Do not keep retrying.\n2. Disconnect everything and confirm correct polarity (+ to +, - to -) before reconnecting anything.\n3. Check the in-line fuse/breaker — a correctly installed fuse often sacrifices itself to protect the battery.\n4. Look/smell for heat damage at terminals, melted insulation, or damaged connected devices.\n\nFIX\n- Replace any blown fuse and re-wire with correct polarity, verified with a meter before powering on.\n- If the battery now reads 0V or won't power on, attempt the standard activation (see the 0V entry).\n- If there's visible damage, heat, a burnt smell, or it won't recover after activation, stop and collect: SN photo, whole-battery photo, a description/photo of what was connected and how, and any fuse/device damage. Escalate — a reverse-polarity event can damage the BMS internally.\n\nWHY IT HAPPENS\nMis-wiring during install or reconnection, unclear terminal marking, or rushing a series/parallel build.\n\nHOW TO AVOID\nAlways verify polarity with a multimeter before connecting. Install a correctly-rated main fuse near the battery (about 1.25× max continuous current) and keep the breaker open while wiring. Use colour-coded cable and double-check series/parallel diagrams before powering up.",
