      "template": "WHAT'S HAPPENING\nA battery terminal has melted, deformed, or collapsed, often with discoloration or a burnt smell.\n\nHOW TO DIAGNOSE\n1. Was the connection tight? Check the OT/ring terminal thickness is >=2mm; if thinner, the included washer should have been added.\n2. Were the spring + flat washers on the screw compressed flat? If not, the joint was effectively loose.\n3. Any oxidation or dirt on the terminal face?\n4. If using crocodile-clip chargers, were the clips properly clamped on tight screws?\n\nFIX\n- Disconnect. Clean any oxidation/dirt; the face must be smooth and clean to reuse.\n- Re-tighten to the manual's torque with a torque wrench; add the packaging washer if the ring terminal is thin.\n- A melted/collapsed terminal usually means the part is damaged — collect SN + whole-battery photo; we replace melted-terminal batteries.\n\nWHY IT HAPPENS\nHigh contact resistance at a loose/oxidised/dirty joint concentrates heat during high-current charge/discharge until the terminal softens and melts.\n\nHOW TO AVOID\nUse correctly-sized ring terminals, the supplied washers, and a torque wrench to spec. Keep terminals clean, and verify clip-style chargers are clamped firmly.",
